     1	# Reproducing the Experiments and Analysis of the Paper
     2	Before running the analysis notebooks:
     3	1. unzip the three zip files within this folder (callgraph_seq.zip, pycg_output.zip, DynaPyt_callgraphs.zip).
     4	2. create a Python virtual environement (Use Python 3.10 or above)
     5	    ```shell
     6	    python3.10 -m venv .venv
     7	    source .venv/bin/activate
     8	    ```
     9	3. Install requirments
    10	    ```shell
    11	    pip install -r requirements.txt
    12	    ```
    13	## 1. DyPyBench Overview (Sec3)
    14	You can reproduce the plots of the overview of the benchmark by running the notebook: [dypybench_overview_plots.ipynb](./dypybench_overview_plots.ipynb)
    15	
    16	## 2. Call Graphs (Sec4.1)
    17	In this part we generate Dynamic Call Graphs using DynaPyt and we also generate Static Call Graphs using PyCG, then we compare them.
    18	
    19	### Step 1. Generating Dynamic Call Graphs
    20	To generate a dynamic call graph for a project (e.g, project 1), the following commands should be executed (note that it takes on average around 4 Hours to complete the generation per project, so run carefully)
    21	
    22	```shell
    23	# Run test (necessary to setup the project)
    24	python3 dypybench.py --test 1
    25	
    26	# Run instrumentation
    27	python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_instrument 1 --dynapyt_file ./text/includes.txt --dynapyt_analysis CallGraph
    28	
    29	# Run the analysis
    30	python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_run 1 --dynapyt_analysis CallGraph
    31	```
    32	Once the the commands finished executed, a json file would appear under the project folder (~/temp/project1).
    33	
    34	### Step 2. Generating Static Call Graphs
    35	To generate a static call graph for a given project, run the following commands (For documentation, check: https://github.com/vitsalis/PyCG):
    36	```shell
    37	# Run test (necessary to setup the project)
    38	python3 dypybench.py --test 1
    39	
    40	# call PyCG
    41	pycg --package project1 $(find project1 -type f -name "*.py") -o project1.json
    42	```
    43	### Step 3. Parsing the generated files and analysing the call graphs
    44	The analysis of callgraphs was done in the notebook: [callgraphs.ipynb](./callgraphs.ipynb)
    45	
    46	## LExecutor (Sec4.2)
    47	
    48	### Step 1. Generate LExecutor traces
    49	
    50	```shell
    51	# Run LExecutor instrumentation
    52	python3 dypybench.py --lex_instrument 1 --lex_file ./text/includes.txt
    53	
    54	# Generate Traces
    55	python3 dypybench.py --lex_test 1
    56	```
    57	A ".h5" file would be generate within the project folder.
    58	
    59	### Step 2. Retraining LExecutor
    60	To retrain LExector, follow the instruction of the [LExecutor repository](https://github.com/michaelpradel/LExecutor/).
    61	
    62	The plot presented in the paper can reproduced by calling the Python script: [generate_lexecutor_acc.py](./generate_lexecutor_acc.py)
    63	
    64	## Mining Specs (Sec4.3)
    65	
    66	### Step 1: Generate Sequences of API calls
    67	Example on project 1.
    68	
    69	```shell
    70	# Run test (necessary to setup the project)
    71	python3 dypybench.py --test 1
    72	
    73	# Run instrumentation
    74	python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_instrument 1 --dynapyt_file ./text/includes.txt --dynapyt_analysis CallGraphSeq
    75	
    76	# Run the analysis
    77	python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_run 1 --dynapyt_analysis CallGraphSeq
    78	```
    79	### Step 2: Patterns Mining
    80	The specification mining is done in the notebooks: [spec_mine.ipynb](./spec_mine.ipynb) and [plots_sec4.3.ipynb](./plots_sec4.3.ipynb)
